      "text": "First, we observe that $\\tan 30^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$. Rewriting 20 and 25 in terms of 30, we have $\\tan 40^\\circ = \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ$ and $\\tan 55^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} + \\tan 25^\\circ$. \n\nNext, we recall the tangent addition formula $\\tan (x + y) = \\dfrac{\\tan x + \\tan y}{1 - \\tan x \\tan y}$. Plugging in $x = 20^\\circ$ and $y = 25^\\circ$, we derive $\\tan 45^\\circ = \\dfrac{\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}$. Clearly, $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} + \\tan 25^\\circ$ acts as the numerator and both sides of this equation work out, establishing a $p$=$r$ equivalence between $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} + \\tan 25^\\circ$ and $\\dfrac{\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}$.\n\nTherefore, the original multiplication simplifies to $\\tan 30^\\circ (\\tan 45^\\circ + \\tan 15^\\circ)$, where $\\tan 15^\\circ = \\tan (45^\\circ - 30^\\circ) = \\dfrac{\\tan 45^\\circ - \\tan 30^\\circ}{1 + \\tan 45^\\circ \\tan 30^\\circ}$. Converting this back to degrees we find $\\tan 15^\\circ = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1}$. Multiplying the denominators and numerators of these expressions yields $\\tan (30^\\circ)(\\tan 45^\\circ + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1}) = \\tan (30^\\circ)(\\boxed{\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}})$. $\\boxed{\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}}$ is the final simplified form.",
